  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "INTERPOL (International Criminal Police Organization) is an intergovernmental organization with 196 member countries that facilitates global police cooperation to fight transnational crimes like terrorism, cybercrime, and organized crime. Based in Lyon, France, it is not a direct law enforcement agency but supports national police through secure communications, databases, and forensic expertise.Key Aspects of INTERPOLMission: To help police worldwide work together to create a safer world.Capabilities: Provides secure communication systems (I-24/7), criminal databases (fingerprints, DNA, stolen documents), and forensic support.Notices: Issues international requests for cooperation, most famously Red Notices to locate and arrest wanted persons pending extradition.Structure: Comprised of a General Secretariat, 196 National Central Bureaus (NCBs), and managed by a General Assembly.Focus Areas: Terrorism, cybercrime, financial crime, corruption, and crimes against children.Key Differences from Popular BeliefNo Independent Power: INTERPOL agents do not have independent police powers, nor can they arrest individuals on their own.Not a Police Force: It acts as a facilitator for national police agencies, not a global police force.What is Not IncludedIt does not intervene in crimes that do not have a transnational aspect (e.g., domestic issues).It is strictly prohibited from engaging in political, military, religious, or racial activities.Note on \"Interpol\" (Band)There is also a well-known American rock band named Interpol, formed in New York City, which is distinct from the international law enforcement organization.",
